    <!-- BOI Report -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-extrabold text-gray-900 sm:text-3xl">Beneficial Ownership Information (BOI) Reporting</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 space-y-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p>
          Under the Corporate Transparency Act, most LLCs formed in the United States — including New York LLCs — must file a Beneficial Ownership Information (BOI) report with the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN). This is a federal requirement, separate from New York's state filings like the Articles of Organization and the publication requirement.
        </p>
        <p>
          The BOI report identifies the individuals who ultimately own or control your LLC. You must report each beneficial owner's full legal name, date of birth, residential address, and a government-issued ID number (such as a driver's license or passport number).
        </p>
        <p><strong>Key deadlines:</strong></p>
        <ul class="ml-6 list-disc space-y-2">
          <li><strong>New LLCs:</strong> Must file within 30 days of formation (for LLCs formed on or after January 1, 2025).</li>
          <li><strong>Existing LLCs:</strong> LLCs formed before January 1, 2025 should check current deadlines, as enforcement timelines have been subject to court challenges and legislative changes.</li>
          <li><strong>Changes:</strong> If your LLC's beneficial ownership information changes (new member, address change, etc.), you must file an updated report within 30 days.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Filing is done online through FinCEN's BOI E-Filing system at no cost. Failure to file can result in civil penalties of up to $591 per day and criminal penalties including fines up to $10,000 and imprisonment.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a complete walkthrough, see our <a href="/learn/boi-report-guide/" class="text-teal-700 underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-teal-900">BOI Report Guide</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

